(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-vignettes"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old man becomes ill over 4 days with a temperature of 40.1 °C (104.2 °F), shaking chills and confusion. He looks toxic, a new loud regurgitant murmur is heard, and painless hemorrhagic macules appear on his soles, which are Janeway lesions (painless, nontender spots). Which form of infective endocarditis fits his course?","choices":["Subacute endocarditis","Libman-Sacks disease","Device endocarditis","Acute endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute endocarditis is a hectically febrile illness from a virulent, invasive organism: rapid onset over 3-10 days, high fevers, systemic toxicity, rapid valve damage and seeding of extracardiac sites such as the skin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman had a mechanical mitral valve placed 4 years ago. She has 5 weeks of low-grade fever and fatigue, and blood cultures are pending. Which organism is most likely in her prosthetic valve infection?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Candida albicans","Escherichia coli"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Late prosthetic valve endocarditis is caused by viridans streptococci, while early infection (around surgery) is caused by coagulase-negative staphylococci and Staphylococcus aureus. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man had his aortic valve replaced 6 weeks ago. He now has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F) and a new regurgitant murmur, and transesophageal imaging shows a vegetation on the prosthesis. Which organisms are most likely?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Enterococci from urine","Airway gram-negatives","Staphylococci from the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Early prosthetic valve endocarditis is caused by coagulase-negative staphylococci (Staphylococcus epidermidis) and Staphylococcus aureus, skin organisms introduced around the time of surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve has a fever of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F) and conjunctival petechiae (pinpoint hemorrhages on the conjunctiva). No blood cultures or echocardiogram have been done yet. How is his case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Endocarditis ruled out","No criteria are met","Possible endocarditis","Definite endocarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He has three minor criteria: a predisposing heart condition (prosthetic valve), fever, and a vascular phenomenon (conjunctival hemorrhages). Three minor criteria make endocarditis possible. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with previous infective endocarditis needs a root canal. She developed hives (urticaria) after amoxicillin years ago and can take pills. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral cephalexin","Oral amoxicillin","Oral clindamycin","Oral azithromycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A penicillin-allergic patient who can take oral medication gets azithromycin or clarithromycin, or doxycycline; cephalexin is avoided after urticaria to penicillin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has 2 months of fever, weight loss and fatigue. A new murmur with a tumor plop (a low-pitched early diastolic sound) is heard, and three sets of blood cultures drawn before any antibiotics are negative. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Polymyalgia rheumatica","Lyme disease","Atrial myxoma","Subacute endocarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial myxoma mimics endocarditis with fever and a new murmur, a tumor plop or mid-diastolic rumble, but it is a tumor and the cultures stay negative. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with mitral valve endocarditis develops worsening breathlessness, crackles and a new loud regurgitant murmur on day 3 of appropriate antibiotics. Echocardiography shows a ruptured chord with severe regurgitation. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery","Addiction medicine","Palliative care alone","Outpatient cardiology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure due to valvular dysfunction is the most common indication for surgery, and cardiothoracic surgery evaluates the need for surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with aortic valve endocarditis develops sharp chest pain and a scratchy friction rub on day 5 of treatment. Imaging shows infection spreading beyond the valve ring. Which process explains the new pericardial findings?","choices":["Abscess extending into the septum","Septic emboli to the coronary arteries","Drug-induced lupus from antibiotics","Abscess extending into the epicardium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A perivalvular abscess that extends into the epicardium causes pericarditis; extension into the upper ventricular septum would instead interrupt conduction. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve needs a dental extraction. He had a mild delayed rash (no hives, swelling or anaphylaxis) with penicillin and cannot take oral medication. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["Clindamycin injection","Cefazolin by injection","Ampicillin injection","Oral azithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin-allergic patients unable to take oral medication receive cefazolin or ceftriaxone intramuscularly or intravenously, provided there is no history of anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has had 2 months of low-grade fevers to 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), fatigue and a 4-kilogram weight loss. She had a tooth extracted 10 weeks ago. A new apical systolic murmur is heard, and splinter hemorrhages (thin dark lines under the nail plates) are seen on two fingers. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Subacute infective endocarditis","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Acute infective endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An indolent course with lower fevers, malaise and weight loss over weeks to months, a new murmur, splinter hemorrhages and a dental source fits subacute endocarditis, classically from viridans streptococci. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve has endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. His son asks how serious this organism is. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["It carries a good prognosis","It rarely needs any surgery","It resolves within a week","It is the most lethal cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus is the most lethal organism in endocarditis, with mortality highest in prosthetic valve endocarditis (about 50%). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"An 8-year-old with endocarditis has a sudden fulminant illness with high spiking fevers, unlike the usual slow course in children. Which organisms does this presentation suggest?","choices":["Candida or other fungi","Pneumococcus or Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans streptococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A fulminant presentation with high spiking fevers suggests Streptococcus pneumoniae or Staphylococcus aureus, while most children have an indolent course. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve placed 3 years ago has endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Vancomycin, rifampin and gentamicin","Nafcillin, rifampin and gentamicin","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","Penicillin G plus gentamicin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus prosthetic valve infection adds rifampin and gentamicin to the antistaphylococcal drug, which is vancomycin when the strain is methicillin-resistant. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man is on day 6 of antibiotics for aortic valve endocarditis. He still has fevers, and today's electrocardiogram shows a new conduction block not present on admission. Which complication best explains the new conduction abnormality?","choices":["Septal perivalvular abscess","Septic emboli to the lungs","Spread into the epicardium","Chordae tendineae rupture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When infection extends beyond the leaflets into the myocardium as a perivalvular abscess and reaches the upper ventricular septum, it interrupts the conduction system. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has 3 days of fever to 40.0 °C (104.0 °F), rigors and a new murmur, and he looks toxic. Which organism is a classic cause of this tempo of infective endocarditis?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Gram-negative coccobacilli","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An abrupt, toxic, high-fever course is acute endocarditis, caused by virulent organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus and beta-hemolytic streptococci. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with a prosthetic mitral valve has a fever of 38.7 °C (101.7 °F). Three blood cultures grow viridans streptococci, and a transesophageal echocardiogram shows new partial dehiscence of the prosthesis. How many major Duke criteria does he meet?","choices":["No major criteria","One major criterion","Two major criteria","Three major criteria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A typical organism in separate cultures is one major criterion, and new partial dehiscence of a prosthetic valve on echocardiogram is the second; two major criteria make the case definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man who injects drugs has a fever of 39.4 °C (102.9 °F) and Janeway lesions (painless hemorrhagic macules) on the soles of both feet. Blood cultures are pending. Under the modified Duke criteria, which category do the Janeway lesions fall into?","choices":["Microbiologic evidence","Vascular phenomenon","Immunologic phenomenon","Major endocardial criterion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Janeway lesions are a vascular (embolic) phenomenon, a minor criterion with major arterial emboli, septic pulmonary infarcts, mycotic aneurysm, intracranial hemorrhage and conjunctival hemorrhages. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with native valve endocarditis is improving on day 4 of intravenous antibiotics and asks whether he can switch to pills so he can go home sooner. Which reply is most accurate?","choices":["Two more days, then stop","Stop when the fever is gone","Therapy stays parenteral","Pills work just as well now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics for endocarditis should be given parenterally and must be bactericidal and prolonged, typically 4-6 weeks from the first negative culture; on day 4 the course has barely begun. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 12-year-old had a ventricular septal defect closed with a prosthetic patch 4 months ago and is now scheduled for a dental extraction. Which is the most appropriate step regarding endocarditis?","choices":["Delay the extraction a year","Give antibiotics afterward","Give prophylaxis beforehand","No prophylaxis is needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The first 6 months after congenital heart disease repair with prosthetic material or a device is a high-risk period, and extraction manipulates the gums, so prophylaxis is given. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 17-year-old who injects drugs has fever, rigors and a tricuspid valve vegetation. Which organism is most likely?","choices":["Candida species","Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans group streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adolescents increasingly resemble adults, including injection drug use-associated Staphylococcus aureus disease, which dominates tricuspid infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man who injects drugs has finished a course of intravenous antibiotics for tricuspid valve endocarditis and is preparing for discharge. He says he wants help to stay safe. Which consultation should be arranged?","choices":["Nephrology","Addiction medicine","Dermatology","Ophthalmology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Addiction medicine is consulted for harm reduction in people who inject drugs, part of the multidisciplinary care with infectious disease and cardiothoracic surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man who injects drugs has tricuspid valve endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus. He has no penicillin allergy. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Nafcillin or oxacillin","Vancomycin or daptomycin","Penicillin G with gentamicin","Ceftriaxone with azithromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus is treated with nafcillin or oxacillin, or cefazolin; vancomycin is the drug for methicillin-resistant strains and only an alternative for susceptible ones. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with mitral valve endocarditis has 2 days of worsening breathlessness and orthopnea (breathlessness when lying flat). A new, loud regurgitant murmur is heard and crackles fill both lung bases. Which mechanism explains her heart failure?","choices":["A toxic myocarditis from fever","Fluid overload from antibiotics","Constriction from the pericardium","Valvular dysfunction from infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Congestive heart failure in infective endocarditis is due to valvular dysfunction, here from leaflet damage or chordal rupture, and it is the most common indication for surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve is having a tooth extracted tomorrow. He has no drug allergies and takes pills without difficulty. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["Clindamycin before the visit","No prophylaxis is needed","Oral amoxicillin, 30-60 minutes before","Oral amoxicillin for 7 days after"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A high-risk patient (prosthetic valve) having an extraction gets a single dose of oral amoxicillin 30-60 minutes before the procedure, targeting viridans streptococci. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman in urgent care has a fever, a new murmur and splinter hemorrhages (dark lines under the nail plates). Before transfer, a colleague proposes giving a dose of ceftriaxone right away. Which action should be avoided?","choices":["Treating before cultures are drawn","Drawing three sets of cultures","Arranging hospital admission","Requesting an echocardiogram"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy is started only after blood cultures have been drawn; antibiotics given first may sterilize the cultures and lose the organism. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man who injects heroin has 4 days of fever to 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), rigors and a cough. A new holosystolic murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border, and he looks toxic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Community-acquired pneumonia","Mitral valve endocarditis","Acute viral pericarditis","Tricuspid valve endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In people who inject drugs the tricuspid valve is the valve singled out (Staphylococcus aureus causes 80-90% of those infections); cough is linked to this group, and a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border fits the tricuspid valve. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man who injects drugs has a fever of 39.0 °C (102.2 °F) and Janeway lesions (painless macules) on his palms. A transesophageal echocardiogram shows an oscillating mass on the tricuspid valve. Blood cultures are still pending. How is his case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Not classifiable yet","Definite endocarditis","Possible endocarditis","Endocarditis ruled out"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. One major criterion (the oscillating mass on echocardiogram) plus three minor criteria (injection drug use as predisposition, fever, and Janeway lesions as a vascular phenomenon) make endocarditis definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman who injects drugs has native valve endocarditis, and her blood cultures grow methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Ampicillin with gentamicin","Vancomycin or daptomycin","Nafcillin or oxacillin","Cefazolin alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is treated with vancomycin or daptomycin, the key difference from susceptible strains being vancomycin in place of a penicillin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with a history of infective endocarditis has a fever of 38.8 °C (101.8 °F), Janeway lesions (painless macules) on her soles and Osler nodes (painful nodules) on two finger pads. One of three blood cultures grows viridans streptococci, the other two are negative, and echocardiography is not yet available. How is her case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Not classifiable yet","Definite endocarditis","Possible endocarditis","Endocarditis ruled out"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She has five minor criteria: predisposition (previous endocarditis), fever, a vascular phenomenon (Janeway lesions), an immunologic phenomenon (Osler nodes) and microbiologic evidence (one positive culture that misses the major criterion). Five minor criteria make the case definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman on long-term immunosuppression has endocarditis, and her blood cultures grow Candida. Which complication is most likely?","choices":["Embolic large artery occlusion","Rapid cure with medication","Isolated rheumatic carditis","Pericardial knock"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In fungal endocarditis the most likely complication is embolism and occlusion of larger peripheral arteries from embolization. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
